{
  "id": "median-of-two-sorted-arrays",
  "title": "Median of Two Sorted Arrays",
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "categories": ["binary-search", "array"],
  "descriptionMd": "You are given two arrays `nums1` and `nums2`, each sorted in non-decreasing order. Return the **median** of all their elements taken together.\n\nThe median is the middle value of the combined sorted list; when the combined length is even, it is the average of the two middle values. One of the arrays may be empty, but not both.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nnums1 = [1, 3], nums2 = [2]\n→ 2.0\n```\nCombined: `[1, 2, 3]`, whose middle value is `2`.\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nnums1 = [1, 2], nums2 = [3, 4]\n→ 2.5\n```\nCombined: `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, so the median is `(2 + 3) / 2`.\n\n**Follow-up:** merging is O(m + n). Can you do it in O(log(min(m, n)))?",
  "constraints": ["0 ≤ nums1.length, nums2.length ≤ 1000", "1 ≤ nums1.length + nums2.length ≤ 2000", "-10⁶ ≤ nums1[i], nums2[i] ≤ 10⁶", "Both arrays are sorted in non-decreasing order"],
  "hints": ["The median splits the combined elements into a left half and a right half of equal size. If you choose how many elements come from `nums1`, the count from `nums2` is forced.", "A split is valid when everything on the left is ≤ everything on the right. Binary search over the count taken from the shorter array."],
  "signature": {
    "functionName": "findMedianSortedArrays",
    "params": [{ "name": "nums1", "type": "int[]" }, { "name": "nums2", "type": "int[]" }],
    "returns": "float"
  },
  "comparator": "float",
